        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T18:49:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When the SYS_RST_ORL releases earlier than the power-off sequence, does it handle correctly?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 387,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 387,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "22ae877e05ac7d81856988e6c461bcba697cb2f8",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c7e9c6c0_e4459cf1",
        "filename": "power/qcom_exp.c",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 387,
      "author": {
        "id": 3391351
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-02T10:09:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it should be handled correctly. here is my thought process: \n\ncase 1: when `sys_rst_odl` de-asserts right after the 20*msec boundary and the power_req is marked to turn off  and not yet executed. \nIn this case since the sys_rst_odl has already de-asserted and the power_req will be overridden to power_req_on, if the system was previously on. so the system state will be preserved and nothing needs to be handled.\n\ncase 2: when `sys_rst_odl` de-asserts right after the 20*msec boundary but the power_off req is already executing but not fully turned off. \n\nIn this case as well the system is powering off and once the `sys_rst_odl` desserts the interrupt will mark the state as `power_req_on` if previously ON, in that case the system will power back up. once the power-off sequence is complete. \n\nlet me know your thoughts. \n\nThanks",
      "parentUuid": "efa84446_b88bb1b3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 387,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 387,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "22ae877e05ac7d81856988e6c461bcba697cb2f8",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "501f8eed_9809abd0",
        "filename": "power/qcom_exp.c",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 387,
      "author": {
        "id": 3391351
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-02T10:14:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One case I can think off where it could fail: \n\ncase3: lets say the power_off_seq is undergoing and the execution is at line number 716 right before `power_request \u003d POWER_REQ_NONE;`, in that case the power_request will be override to NONE and the previous power on state might not be preserved. to solve this I think the power_req clean-up for `POWER_REQ_OFF_LONG_WARM_RESET` needs to be handled separately. \n\ni\u0027ll update that.",
      "parentUuid": "c7e9c6c0_e4459cf1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 387,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 387,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "22ae877e05ac7d81856988e6c461bcba697cb2f8",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
